# CH09 — A Voice with My Hands. Nacre and the Memory Vault. SC06 is authored.

@scene T06_SEN
if ch:CH09,!flag:accord_nacre accord
if ch:CH09 after
say sen | Archivist Sen. Nacre keeps disagreements. Other archives keep only the winner's copy.
say sen | The Memory Vault is north, up the vault road. Go in with a question, not an answer.
end
label accord
call CH10_NACRE
end
label after
say sen | You kept both accounts. Good. The record is heavier than it was. That's how you know it's honest.
@end

@scene T06_GARDENER
say farmer | Do the plants count as sacred? No idea. They need water either way.
@end

@scene T06_FAMILY
say elder | The pool showed my grandmother's wedding. My granddaughter saw a funeral in the same water.
say survivor | We saw the same morning. It was both. We argue about it every week. It's our favourite argument.
@end

@scene T06_SALT
say keeper | Salt, jars, remedies. The good cloth-lined jars keep water sweet across the flats.
shop SHOP_T06
@end

@scene T06_WATER
say worker | Water carried in cloth-lined jars. It tastes of linen. You get used to it.
@end

@scene T06_PILGRIM
say monk | I came to hear my mother in the pool. I heard three other people's mothers. It helped anyway.
@end

@scene T06_INN
say monk | Quiet cloister. We ask little and you rest long. No talking after the second bell.
inn
@end

@scene T06_LISTENER
say scholar | Listening is a practice. You don't take from the pool. You let it tell you, and you're allowed to stop.
@end

@scene T06_POOL_KEEPER
say sen | The listening pool. It holds voices that were never anyone's property. Come back later. It will still be here.
@end

@scene D08_CONSENT once
say narr | The Listening Gate. Words cut in the lintel: 'The vault will show you memory, if you consent. You may refuse. The walls carry the same testimony in ink.'
say oriel | It is asking. That is more than the ministry ever did.
choice | Accept the memory projection -> accept | Decline; read the walls instead -> decline
label accept
set d08_projection
say narr | A cool pressure behind the eyes, then light. The vault will show you what it holds.
goto done
label decline
set d08_documents
say narr | You decline. The gate dims politely. Ink testimony lines every wall ahead.
label done
say narr | (Your choice changes how the vault presents its testimony, not what you learn.)
objective oriel | Gather the three testimonies and set them in order on the chronology wall in the Voluntary Accord.
@end

@scene D08_T1
if flag:d08_t1 heard
set d08_t1
if flag:d08_projection mem
doc Testimony_I | 312 years ago, a regional disaster. Some humans and dragons chose to share bodies to survive it. Each accord was voluntary. Each included a separation rite, available to either party at any time.
end
label mem
say narr | Memory: two voices in one throat, laughing about the weather. One of them says, 'Whenever you want to leave, say so.'
end
label heard
say narr | Testimony I: the voluntary accords, with a separation rite.
@end

@scene D08_T2A
if flag:d08_t2a heard
set d08_t2a
doc Testimony_II_(worker) | A regulator crew chief: 'They said the hosts were infrastructure now. We stopped using names on the shift boards. The separation rite was struck from the training manual.'
end
label heard
say narr | Testimony II, first account: the rite was removed; hosts became infrastructure.
@end

@scene D08_T2B
if flag:d08_t2b heard
set d08_t2b
doc Testimony_II_(minister) | A ministry order: 'Standardisation improves safety. Separation rites caused outages. Discontinuation is humane in aggregate.'
say tessa | 'Humane in aggregate.' That's a sentence you'd only write if you knew it was a lie.
say oriel | Or if you believed it. Both accounts go in the record.
end
label heard
say narr | Testimony II, second account: the ministry's reasons for standardisation.
@end

@scene D08_T3
if flag:d08_t3 heard
set d08_t3
if flag:d08_projection mem
doc Testimony_III | A century ago: the last independent great dragons were broken into distributed consciousnesses, spread across bred lineages and relay organs. The industry called the organs fossils. The lineages were called hosts.
end
label mem
say narr | Memory flickers toward a nursery ledger. Dain stops at the records and does not step into the named child's memory.
say dain | Records only. It's hers.
doc Testimony_III | A century ago: the last independent great dragons were broken into distributed consciousnesses, spread across bred lineages and relay organs. The industry called the organs fossils. The lineages were called hosts.
end
label heard
say narr | Testimony III: engineered lineages and distributed consciousness.
@end

@scene D08_WALL
if flag:d08_chrono done
if !flag:d08_t1 missing
if !flag:d08_t2a missing
if !flag:d08_t2b missing
if !flag:d08_t3 missing
say narr | The chronology wall has three slots. Which testimony goes first?
choice | The industrial standardisation -> wrong | The voluntary accords -> first | The engineered lineages -> wrong
label first
say narr | Second?
choice | The engineered lineages -> wrong | The standardisation (both accounts) -> second | Only the ministry's account -> onevoice
label second
say narr | Third?
choice | The engineered lineages -> third | Leave it empty -> wrong
label third
sfx FX024
set d08_chrono
say narr | The wall accepts the record: voluntary accords; their standardisation, in two voices; the engineered lineages. The choir door beyond the galleries opens.
say sen | (A voice from far above, carried by the vault): Heavier than it was.
objective | The Choir Chamber is open, north of the First Industry and the Quiet Nursery.
end
label onevoice
sfx FX004
say narr | The wall rejects it. A record with one voice removed is not a record. The tablets return to their shelves.
end
label wrong
sfx FX004
say narr | Dissonance. The tablets slide back to their shelves. (The dates on each tablet show the order.)
end
label missing
say narr | The wall needs three testimonies, and both accounts of the second. (The Voluntary Accord, the First Industry, the Quiet Nursery.)
end
label done
say narr | The chronology is set.
@end

@scene D08_CHOIR once
music silence
say narr | The Choir Chamber. Three masked figures sing one note so perfectly that it hurts to hear.
say "Pale Choir" | ONE HISTORY. ONE ACCOUNT. REMOVE THE CONTRADICTION.
say oriel | It wants to make every account agree. Vary your commands - it echoes whatever you repeat.
battle B08 noflee
set b08_done
music M024
say narr | The masks crack. The single note breaks into many voices, all different, none silenced.
say narr | A small fox made of warm light steps out of the broken chorus and sits at Oriel's feet.
say "Lumen Fox" | You kept every voice. I light what is actually there. Call me when you need to see.
vestige V05
xp 380
@end

@scene D08_SC06 once
say narr | The Still Pool. Dain kneels at the water. His reflection moves a half-beat late.
say ilyr | There is enough of me here to begin again.
say dain | Here means my hands.
say ilyr | They made them to hold me.
say dain | They are still mine.
say ilyr | You would keep me imprisoned.
say dain | I would stop you calling my life an empty room.
say oriel | There was a way to leave. Before they removed it.
say dain | Then we find that way.
say ilyr | And until then?
say dain | You ask.
wait 0.6
say ilyr | ...I am asking to speak with you. Not through you.
say dain | Yes.
set ilyr_accord
say tessa | So the heartglass organs are relays, and dragonborn are - memory nodes. Living ones.
say rook | And the relays can be released. All at once, from the Crown Dais.
say oriel | 'Released' would include every dragonborn host. Every node. At once.
say rook | Freedom for all of them.
say oriel | Without asking any of them.
chapter CH09
objective oriel | Return the evidence to Cinderwake, High Aerie and Nacre. Each needs something different.
rumor | Ivo and Oriel think a single relay could be released slowly, locally, without a captive holding it.
level_floor 20
@end
